Let’s get into it.

The attorney you choose to represent you is the single biggest difference-maker throughout the [California lemon law process](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law). To reiterate, this is probably your first brush with lemon law, or for that matter, any civil case. As much as we’d love to tell you that ALL attorneys (let alone lemon law attorneys) are straight shooters, this simply may not be the case.

When [searching for a lemon law lawyer](/blog/find-trustworthy-california-lemon-law-attorney) in California, there are a handful of red flags that should leave you searching for another option. Here are some of the big ones:

## A. They Aren’t Specialized in CA lemon Law Process

Lemon law exists across the country, but the details differ from [state to state](https://www.dmv.org/automotive-law/lemon-law.php). The lemon law process in California is unique, and you need a seasoned attorney who knows them backward and forward. Moreover, big automakers spare no expense in their legal teams. A general practice lawyer will likely get chewed up by the manufacturer’s defense efforts.

Generally speaking, if the attorney doesn’t specifically advertise California lemon law services, they probably won’t be able to earn you a fast, comprehensive buyback.

## B. They Are a Big, Nationwide Firm

While they may advertise specific California lemon law services, big nationwide firms are not usually the best choice. Most offer subpar services with business models typically based on client volume.

This means they will sign a new client and then pass the case off to an assistant or paralegal. In most cases, this ends with the consumer getting a low-ball cash settlement from the manufacturer rather than a full buyback. So, when you shop for a lemon law lawyer in California, always go with a specialized California-based firm.

## C. They Don’t Have Much Experience with the Manufacturer of Your Vehicle

This is a big one. As mentioned earlier, automakers have access to the most expensive legal defense teams money can buy. Each manufacturer has its own set of hurdles and challenges in the CA lemon law process. You need an attorney who has dealt with them before and knows how to navigate around the obstacles.

When vetting a lemon law attorney in California, ask them if they’ve handled cases with your particular vehicle manufacturer. It would help if you also asked them about their unique challenges in the lemon law process. If they can’t provide solid answers or show you any case studies/past examples, it’s probably time to find the door.

## D. They Make Bold Guarantees in the Consultation

There are very few guarantees in the CA lemon law process - this applies to pretty much any area of law. A trustworthy lemon law lawyer won’t make any promises of a buyback or replacement in the initial consultation - because they can’t.

Generally speaking, a good lawyer will be cautiously optimistic in your consultation. However, if they are making promises about successful lemon law claims and the lemon law process that seem too good to be true, keep looking.

## E. They Demand Fees from You Upfront

Much like personal injury cases, clients are in this predicament through no fault of their own. A lemon is the result of a manufacturer’s mistakes in the production process. Consumers affected by this should NEVER have to pay out-of-pocket costs to seek justice.

When you win your case, the manufacturer is legally required to pay for ALL legal costs and attorney fees. A good lemon law lawyer in California knows this and won’t ask you for anything out of pocket. Additionally, this puts the pressure on them to earn a speedy buyback. They will not take your case unless they think they can win.

## 2. Totaling Up Incidental Costs

California lemon law challenges extend much further than the vehicle itself.

Incidental costs are all the collateral damages that stemmed from the lemon. The manufacturer is legally obligated to reimburse you for these costs as part of the buyback. This is in addition to the cost of the vehicle, down payment, loan payments, attorney fees, court costs, etc.

The ripple effect of a lemon can be significant. These incidental costs would include everything like towing costs, cab fares, hotel stays, meals, repair expenses, lost wages, and so on.

Tallying up all the costs for the lemon law process in California can get confusing. This is why it’s so vitally important that you keep records of every single expenditure tied to the lemon. You will need to give all of this to your attorney so they can negotiate the buyback amount during the lemon law California process. Losing a receipt or not keeping records is essentially like flushing money down the toilet.

## 3. Keeping Your Emotional State in Check

One of the last things you want to do is jump at the first offer from a manufacturer. It’s not uncommon for them to low-ball you out of frustration, hoping that your desire for a quick resolution will make you cave. In fact, nearly all manufacturers are more likely to go low to get you out of the equation.

They want to minimize their financial liability, so it’s in your interest for a reasonable settlement that covers all costs and fees. Your attorney will be able to help you navigate this part of the lemon law process in California. Emotional reactions can [negatively impact negotiations](/blog/factors-influence-ability-win-lemon-law-case) and even the amount you’re entitled to.

## 4. Being Patient, But Still Proactive

[Lemon law cases](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law) take time to resolve. Manufacturers have no problem [dragging their feet](/blog/automakers-avoid-manufacturer-buyback) on the buyback, hoping that consumers will give up and walk away.

Don’t let them win. It’s important to keep in mind that your case matters, and it WILL be resolved. You’ve done all the legwork - made it through the consultation and got a good lawyer. Let your attorney handle the details and work towards getting you a fair settlement.

## 5. Filing a Claim for a Used Vehicle

Used vehicles and the lemon law process can get tricky, which brings us to the final California lemon law challenge.

Most states only have lemon laws for _new_ cars. Fortunately, California is not one of them.

Owners of used vehicles may [file a lemon law claim](/info/everything-you-need-to-know-about-california-lemon-law-claims) as long as the vehicle was sold with the balance of the manufacturer’s new car warranty. This is most common in vehicles sold as certified pre-owned. Additionally, the lemon law will apply if the car is sold with a dealer warranty - which may be good for 30 days after the purchase date or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer.

The [dealer warranty](/blog/is-your-vehicles-warranty-valid-for-a-california-lemon-law-claim) should have been displayed on the buyer’s guide when you purchased the used vehicle. If the vehicle was NOT sold with a warranty, it should be clearly labeled that it was sold “as is.”

If you bought the vehicle “as is,” it means you may be assuming full responsibility for anything wrong with the vehicle. As a result, you could lose your legal right to the lemon law process.

Now, in some instances, the buyer’s guide might not specify whether the vehicle is covered under warranty OR if it’s being sold “as is.” If there is no specification, the vehicle is automatically sold with an implied warranty of merchantability. [Under CA law](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&amp&sectionNum=1791.1.), this warranty may be good for at least 60 days after the purchase date.

The window to file a claim for a used vehicle can be very small. The biggest challenge is simply taking action quickly. An experienced lemon law lawyer in California deals with used lemons every day. If you even have the slightest hunch your newly-acquired pre-owned vehicle is defective, don’t waste any time in starting the California lemon law process!

